// teach 5: add with !Alloc
// The clause that stands on `f` is two calls up: `g` declares `!Alloc` and
// calls `h`, whose body calls `f` under no clause of its own. `g`'s proof
// rests on `f` through `h`, so `f` is taught the lock `g` stands on.
fn f(x) = x + 1

fn h(x) = f(x) * 2

fn g(x) with !Alloc = h(x)

fn main() = g(1)
